# Can a strange loop be built from HMD cards?

**The question.** Attempt to build a strange loop using HyperMarkDown cards
that can be dynamically executed with human feedback. This is the third of the
five questions in the project README.

## Where the answer stands

The starting proposal was direct: cards hold knowledge, cells in the cards
compute, the output is written beside the cell, and Git records each revision.
Run that repeatedly and the document changes itself.

The discussion's first finding is that this is not yet a strange loop. A cycle
of document, code, output and document again is ordinary feedback, however
often it runs. The second finding sharpens the target. Output can itself be
Markdown containing new cells, so data becomes code and code becomes data. That
makes the system a reflective programming substrate. It still qualifies as a
strange loop only when cross-level self-reference is actually shown.

What would have to be shown is that a card represents the mechanism that
interprets it, and that the interpretation feeds back into that same
mechanism. Three designs approach this from different sides.

- **The observer card.** A card refers to itself, a cell inspects a pinned
  revision of it, the evaluation emits structured observations, a person or
  agent proposes an edit to the card or to its rules of interpretation, and
  Git records the accepted revision.
- **Render, integrate, execute.** Three operations kept apart, with approval
  between them, so that generated text never runs itself.
- **The Gödel construction in HQL.** Syntax trees, an encoding, substitution,
  proof checking and diagonalization, implemented as a program. The warning
  attached is that executing the construction from outside is not the same as
  proving that a theory represents it from inside.

Two results from the mathematics shape the experiment. Neither exact example
of self-description needs feedback over time, so a loop that merely runs is
beside the point. And a human who adds axioms, or revises the criteria for
adding them, gives a loop that evolves, though a program could play that part.

This vault is itself the first material for the experiment. Its cards define
level crossing and feedback, and its future lenses would ask whether its own
link cycles satisfy those definitions.

